{"bendscript":"0.1","blocks":[{"id":"blk-75645","kind":"heading","level":1,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107044","text":"Skill 07 — Cognitive Science Foundations"}]},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75647","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107045","text":"Research grounding for each of the ten protocols: the theories, the papers, the design constraints, and the open questions. Eight cognitive primitives grounded in cognitive science / neuroscience, plus two cross-cutting protocols (OS-009 PRISM, OS-010 PULSE) grounded in psychometrics and closed-loop control theory."}]}],"id":"blk-75646","kind":"quote"},{"id":"blk-75648","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107046","text":"Why This Matters"}]},{"id":"blk-75649","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107047","text":"OpenSentience protocols are not arbitrary architecture decisions. Each one maps to a specific cognitive science finding about how intelligent systems manage knowledge, attention, and self-regulation. Understanding the research helps you understand "},{"id":"spn-107048","marks":["italic"],"text":"why"},{"id":"spn-107049","text":" the protocols work the way they do and where the boundaries of the analogy lie."}]},{"id":"blk-75650","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107050","text":"OS-001: Hippocampal Consolidation"}]},{"id":"blk-75651","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107051","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-107052","text":" McClelland, McNaughton & O'Reilly (1995). \"Why there are complementary learning systems in the hippocampus and neocortex.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75652","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107053","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107054","text":" The brain uses two complementary systems for memory. The hippocampus rapidly encodes new experiences (fast write). The neocortex slowly integrates this knowledge into long-term structure (consolidation). Replaying memories during sleep transfers hippocampal traces into neocortical representations without catastrophic forgetting."}]},{"id":"blk-75653","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107055","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-001:"}]},{"id":"blk-75654","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75656","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107056","text":"New knowledge must be stored immediately without disrupting existing memory"}]}],"id":"blk-75655","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75658","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107057","text":"Background consolidation must merge, decay, and prune to maintain quality"}]}],"id":"blk-75657","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75660","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107058","text":"Episodic memories (events) are stored differently from semantic memories (facts)"}]}],"id":"blk-75659","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75662","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107059","text":"Confidence decays over time unless reinforced by retrieval or outcomes"}]}],"id":"blk-75661","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75663","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107060","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75664","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75666","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107061","text":"Immediate "},{"id":"spn-107062","marks":["code"],"text":"store_node"},{"id":"spn-107063","text":" writes (hippocampal fast-write analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-75665","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75668","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107064","marks":["code"],"text":"run_consolidation"},{"id":"spn-107065","text":" background cycle (neocortical integration analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-75667","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75670","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107066","text":"Three node types mapping to memory systems: episodic, semantic, procedural"}]}],"id":"blk-75669","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75671","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107067","text":"OS-002: Working Memory Gating"}]},{"id":"blk-75672","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107068","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-107069","text":" O'Reilly & Frank (2006). \"Making working memory work: A computational model of learning in the prefrontal cortex and basal ganglia.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75673","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107070","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107071","text":" The prefrontal cortex maintains active representations in working memory, but not everything gets in. The basal ganglia acts as a gate — deciding what information is relevant enough to enter working memory based on learned relevance signals and conflict detection."}]},{"id":"blk-75674","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107072","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-002:"}]},{"id":"blk-75675","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75677","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107073","text":"Not all retrieved knowledge should be acted upon equally"}]}],"id":"blk-75676","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75679","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107074","text":"Cyclic knowledge (contradictions, self-reinforcing claims) needs detection"}]}],"id":"blk-75678","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75681","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107075","text":"The kappa parameter serves as the conflict signal: kappa = 0 means no"}]}],"id":"blk-75680","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75682","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107076","text":"conflict (open the gate), kappa > 0 means conflict (engage deliberation)"}]},{"id":"blk-75683","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75685","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107077","text":"Routing must be fast for the common case (acyclic) and thorough for the"}]}],"id":"blk-75684","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75686","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107078","text":"rare case (cyclic)"}]},{"id":"blk-75687","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107079","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75688","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75690","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107080","text":"SCC detection on retrieved neighborhoods (basal ganglia conflict signal)"}]}],"id":"blk-75689","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75692","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107081","text":"Kappa computation (gating threshold)"}]}],"id":"blk-75691","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75694","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107082","text":"Fast vs deliberate routing based on kappa value"}]}],"id":"blk-75693","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75695","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107083","text":"OS-003: Dual-Process Theory"}]},{"id":"blk-75696","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107084","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-107085","text":" Kahneman (2011). \"Thinking, Fast and Slow.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75697","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107086","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107087","text":" Human cognition operates in two modes. System 1 is fast, automatic, and heuristic-based — it handles routine decisions effortlessly. System 2 is slow, effortful, and analytical — it engages when System 1 encounters novelty, conflict, or high stakes."}]},{"id":"blk-75698","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107088","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-003:"}]},{"id":"blk-75699","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75701","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107089","text":"Most agent decisions should be fast (System 1 analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-75700","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75703","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107090","text":"Deliberation should engage only when triggered by conflict signals (kappa > 0)"}]}],"id":"blk-75702","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75705","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107091","text":"The deliberation protocol must structure argumentation (bid, debate, vote,"}]}],"id":"blk-75704","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75706","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107092","text":"commit) to resolve conflict rather than amplify it"}]},{"id":"blk-75707","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75709","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107093","text":"Timeout mechanisms prevent deliberation from stalling indefinitely"}]}],"id":"blk-75708","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75710","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107094","marks":["bold"],"text":"What AgenTroMatic implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75711","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75713","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107095","text":"Four-phase deliberation pipeline (bid/debate/vote/commit)"}]}],"id":"blk-75712","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75715","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107096","text":"Argumentation framework with claims, warrants, and rebuttals"}]}],"id":"blk-75714","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75717","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107097","text":"Consensus and escalation mechanisms"}]}],"id":"blk-75716","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75718","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107098","text":"OS-004: Endogenous Attention"}]},{"id":"blk-75719","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107099","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-107100","text":" Desimone & Duncan (1995). \"Neural mechanisms of selective visual attention.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75720","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107101","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107102","text":" Attention is not just stimulus-driven (exogenous — a loud noise grabs your attention). It is also goal-directed (endogenous — you look for your car keys because you intend to drive). Endogenous attention biases perception toward goal-relevant information, filtering out distractions."}]},{"id":"blk-75721","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107103","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-004:"}]},{"id":"blk-75722","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75724","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107104","text":"The attention engine must prioritize based on active goals, not just recency"}]}],"id":"blk-75723","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75725","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107105","text":"or novelty"}]},{"id":"blk-75726","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75728","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107106","text":"Salience scoring combines urgency, recency, goal relevance, and confidence"}]}],"id":"blk-75727","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75730","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107107","text":"The survey/triage/dispatch cycle mirrors the scan/filter/act structure of"}]}],"id":"blk-75729","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75731","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107108","text":"biological attention"}]},{"id":"blk-75732","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75734","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107109","text":"Dispatch modes (act, learn, escalate, idle) prevent attention from always"}]}],"id":"blk-75733","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75735","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107110","text":"demanding action"}]},{"id":"blk-75736","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107111","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75737","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75739","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107112","marks":["code"],"text":"attention_survey"},{"id":"spn-107113","text":" (endogenous scan of goal-relevant state)"}]}],"id":"blk-75738","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75741","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107114","marks":["code"],"text":"attention_run_cycle"},{"id":"spn-107115","text":" (full survey/triage/dispatch loop)"}]}],"id":"blk-75740","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75743","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107116","text":"Goal bias in salience scoring"}]}],"id":"blk-75742","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75744","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107117","text":"OS-005: Resource Rationality"}]},{"id":"blk-75745","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107118","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-107119","text":" Lieder & Griffiths (2020). \"Resource-rational analysis: Understanding human cognition as the optimal use of limited computational resources.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75746","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107120","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107121","text":" Optimal cognition is not about always computing the best answer. It is about allocating computational effort proportional to the value of the decision. Simple decisions warrant fast, cheap heuristics. High-stakes decisions justify expensive, thorough analysis."}]},{"id":"blk-75747","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107122","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-005:"}]},{"id":"blk-75748","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75750","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107123","text":"Three model tiers map to three levels of computational investment"}]}],"id":"blk-75749","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75752","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107124","text":"The same tool surface and topology are available at every tier"}]}],"id":"blk-75751","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75754","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107125","text":"Escalation rules define when to upgrade from cheap to expensive processing"}]}],"id":"blk-75753","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75756","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107126","text":"Budget constraints (tokens, latency, cost) prevent unbounded computation"}]}],"id":"blk-75755","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75757","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107127","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous and Agentelic implement:"}]},{"id":"blk-75758","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75760","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107128","text":"local_small / local_large / cloud_frontier tier definitions"}]}],"id":"blk-75759","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75762","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107129","text":"Escalation thresholds based on confidence and decision stakes"}]}],"id":"blk-75761","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75764","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107130","text":"Token and latency budgets per tier"}]}],"id":"blk-75763","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75765","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107131","text":"OS-006: Executive Function"}]},{"id":"blk-75766","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107132","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-107133","text":" Miyake, Friedman et al. (2000). \"The unity and diversity of executive functions and their contributions to complex frontal lobe tasks.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75767","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107134","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107135","text":" Executive function is an umbrella term for the cognitive processes that regulate, control, and manage other cognitive processes. Miyake et al. identified three core components: inhibitory control (suppressing inappropriate responses), task switching (flexibly shifting between tasks), and working memory updating (monitoring and revising held information)."}]},{"id":"blk-75768","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107136","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-006:"}]},{"id":"blk-75769","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75771","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107137","text":"Inhibitory control maps to the permission system (blocking disallowed actions)"}]}],"id":"blk-75770","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75773","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107138","text":"Task switching maps to the lifecycle state machine (managing agent state)"}]}],"id":"blk-75772","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75775","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107139","text":"Working memory updating maps to graduated autonomy (adapting the level of"}]}],"id":"blk-75774","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75776","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107140","text":"agent independence based on accumulated trust)"}]},{"id":"blk-75777","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75779","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107141","text":"The audit trail provides metacognitive monitoring — awareness of what the"}]}],"id":"blk-75778","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75780","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107142","text":"system has done"}]},{"id":"blk-75781","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107143","marks":["bold"],"text":"What `open_sentience` implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-75782","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75784","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107144","text":"PermissionEngine (inhibitory control)"}]}],"id":"blk-75783","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75786","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107145","text":"AgentLifecycle GenStateMachine (task switching)"}]}],"id":"blk-75785","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75788","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107146","text":"AutonomyController (working memory updating / cognitive flexibility)"}]}],"id":"blk-75787","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75790","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107147","text":"AuditWriter (metacognitive monitoring)"}]}],"id":"blk-75789","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75791","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107148","text":"OS-007: Adversarial Robustness — Self/Non-Self Discrimination"}]},{"id":"blk-75792","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107149","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theoretical grounding:"},{"id":"spn-107150","text":" Adaptive immunity theory (Burnet 1959; Janeway 1989)."}]},{"id":"blk-75793","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107151","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107152","text":" Biological immune systems distinguish self from non-self through a combination of innate pattern recognition and adaptive memory. The same architecture maps cleanly to agent threat detection: known-good behavior is \"self,\" novel attack patterns are \"non-self,\" and the system must learn to recognize new threats without misclassifying legitimate variation."}]},{"id":"blk-75794","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107153","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-007:"}]},{"id":"blk-75795","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75797","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107154","text":"Five threat categories with explicit detection rules"}]}],"id":"blk-75796","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75799","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107155","text":"Defense protocols must be reversible (quarantine before destroy)"}]}],"id":"blk-75798","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75801","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107156","text":"Integration with OS-006 for permission revocation and OS-008 for circuit"}]}],"id":"blk-75800","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75802","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107157","text":"breaking — the immune response is enacted by the governance and harness layers"}]},{"id":"blk-75803","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107158","text":"OS-008: Supervisory Attentional System"}]},{"id":"blk-75804","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107159","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-107160","text":" Norman & Shallice (1986). \"Attention to action: Willed and automatic control of behavior.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75805","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107161","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107162","text":" Routine behavior runs automatically through contention scheduling between learned action schemas. A "},{"id":"spn-107163","marks":["italic"],"text":"supervisory attentional system"},{"id":"spn-107164","text":" intervenes when novel, dangerous, or constraint-violating situations arise. The supervisory system does not execute behavior directly — it modulates which schemas are allowed to run."}]},{"id":"blk-75806","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107165","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-008:"}]},{"id":"blk-75807","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75809","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107166","text":"The harness is the runtime that calls the agent, not a tool the agent calls"}]}],"id":"blk-75808","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75811","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107167","text":"It enforces pipeline ordering, quality gates, sprint contracts, and context"}]}],"id":"blk-75810","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75812","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107168","text":"management"}]},{"id":"blk-75813","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75815","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107169","text":"It intervenes when prerequisites are not met or when quality thresholds are"}]}],"id":"blk-75814","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75816","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107170","text":"not crossed — but it does not generate the agent's outputs"}]},{"id":"blk-75817","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107171","text":"OS-009: PRISM — Meta-Cognition + Psychometrics"}]},{"id":"blk-75818","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107172","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key references:"}]},{"id":"blk-75819","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75821","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107173","text":"Rasch, G. (1960). \"Probabilistic models for some intelligence and attainment"}]}],"id":"blk-75820","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75822","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107174","text":"tests.\" (Item Response Theory foundation)"}]},{"id":"blk-75823","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75825","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107175","text":"Lord, F. M. (1980). \"Applications of item response theory to practical testing"}]}],"id":"blk-75824","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75826","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107176","text":"problems.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75827","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75829","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107177","text":"Green, D. M. & Swets, J. A. (1966). \"Signal detection theory and"}]}],"id":"blk-75828","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75830","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107178","text":"psychophysics.\""}]},{"id":"blk-75831","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75833","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107179","text":"Flavell, J. H. (1979). \"Metacognition and cognitive monitoring.\" (Meta-cognition)"}]}],"id":"blk-75832","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75834","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107180","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107181","text":" Measuring whether a system "},{"id":"spn-107182","marks":["italic"],"text":"learns"},{"id":"spn-107183","text":" (rather than merely "},{"id":"spn-107184","marks":["italic"],"text":"answers"},{"id":"spn-107185","text":") requires the same toolkit psychometricians built for measuring human learning: calibrated item difficulty, separation of item-quality from learner-quality parameters, and detection of response bias. PRISM applies IRT to scenario calibration and signal detection theory to dimension scoring, then closes the meta-cognitive loop by reflecting on its own scenarios and evolving them."}]},{"id":"blk-75835","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107186","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-009:"}]},{"id":"blk-75836","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75838","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107187","text":"9 continual-learning dimensions (retention, plasticity, transfer,"}]}],"id":"blk-75837","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75839","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107188","text":"contradiction handling, etc.) — each with calibrated scoring rubrics"}]},{"id":"blk-75840","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75842","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107189","text":"4-phase evaluation loop: compose → interact → observe → reflect → diagnose"}]}],"id":"blk-75841","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75843","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107190","text":"(the diagnostic must itself be a closed loop)"}]},{"id":"blk-75844","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75846","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107191","text":"BYOR ingestion — point PRISM at any repo and it generates scenarios"}]}],"id":"blk-75845","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75848","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107192","text":"IRT calibration of scenario difficulty across cycles"}]}],"id":"blk-75847","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75850","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107193","marks":["bold"],"text":"PULSE-aware:"},{"id":"spn-107194","text":" PRISM's "},{"id":"spn-107195","marks":["code"],"text":"interact"},{"id":"spn-107196","text":" phase reads any system's PULSE manifest"}]}],"id":"blk-75849","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75851","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107197","text":"at runtime and drives the inner loop through its declared phases"}]},{"id":"blk-75852","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107198","text":"OS-010: PULSE — Closed-Loop Control Theory + Temporal Cognition"}]},{"id":"blk-75853","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107199","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key references:"}]},{"id":"blk-75854","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75856","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107200","text":"Wiener, N. (1948). \"Cybernetics: or Control and Communication in the Animal"}]}],"id":"blk-75855","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75857","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107201","text":"and the Machine.\" (Closed-loop control foundation)"}]},{"id":"blk-75858","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75860","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107202","text":"Allen, J. F. (1983). \"Maintaining knowledge about temporal intervals.\""}]}],"id":"blk-75859","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75861","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107203","text":"(Interval algebra)"}]},{"id":"blk-75862","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75864","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107204","text":"CloudEvents v1 specification (CNCF, 2019). (Event envelope standard)"}]}],"id":"blk-75863","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75865","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107205","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-107206","text":" Wiener's cybernetics established that intelligent behavior — in animals or machines — requires closed feedback loops with explicit phases: sense, decide, act, observe, adjust. Allen's interval algebra formalized the 13 possible relationships between temporal intervals, providing a vocabulary for describing how loops can nest, overlap, and signal one another. PULSE combines these into a manifest standard: every loop in the [&] portfolio declares its phases in the same vocabulary, and cross-loop signals use CloudEvents v1 envelopes so that loops can compose without bespoke adapters."}]},{"id":"blk-75866","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107207","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-010:"}]},{"id":"blk-75867","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75869","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107208","text":"5 canonical phase kinds ("},{"id":"spn-107209","marks":["code"],"text":"retrieve"},{"id":"spn-107210","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107211","marks":["code"],"text":"route"},{"id":"spn-107212","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107213","marks":["code"],"text":"act"},{"id":"spn-107214","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107215","marks":["code"],"text":"learn"},{"id":"spn-107216","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107217","marks":["code"],"text":"consolidate"},{"id":"spn-107218","text":")"}]}],"id":"blk-75868","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75870","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107219","text":"cover the closed-loop control archetype; custom phases extend it without breaking the schema"}]},{"id":"blk-75871","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75873","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107220","text":"5 canonical cross-loop tokens ("},{"id":"spn-107221","marks":["code"],"text":"TopologyContext"},{"id":"spn-107222","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107223","marks":["code"],"text":"DeliberationResult"},{"id":"spn-107224","text":","}]}],"id":"blk-75872","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75874","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107225","marks":["code"],"text":"OutcomeSignal"},{"id":"spn-107226","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107227","marks":["code"],"text":"ReputationUpdate"},{"id":"spn-107228","text":", "},{"id":"spn-107229","marks":["code"],"text":"ConsolidationEvent"},{"id":"spn-107230","text":") cover the observed inter-system signaling needs"}]},{"id":"blk-75875","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75877","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107231","text":"7 invariants (phase atomicity, feedback immutability, append-only audit,"}]}],"id":"blk-75876","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75878","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107232","text":"kappa routing, quorum before commit, outcome grounding, trace ID propagation) encode the structural correctness conditions"}]},{"id":"blk-75879","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-75881","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-107233","text":"A 12-test conformance suite makes \"PULSE-conforming\" objectively verifiable"}]}],"id":"blk-75880","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-75882","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-107234","text":"Open Research Questions"}]}